On Thu, 2 Mar 2006, Ian-Keith wrote:
> Gary Olson wrote:
>> Sand would appear as brown # or . or ~ and sand dunes would look like
>> \your suggestion here\

   I'd say . is the right character (to match floor and ice, also 
traversable; and different from lava and water, not traversable).
I don't think sand dunes are necessary at all; they don't seem to add
anything to gameplay, and would be suited more to a "desert level"
than just standing alone somewhere in the dungeon.

>> Sand would slow movement for all creatures by half -- except long worms
>> which would speed up. Dunes would slow movement by half again.
>> Jumping would allow movement at normal jumping speed.
>> Dust vortexi and air elementals would do extra damage -- possibly
>> causing rot and destroying paper. Walking thru sand causes rot to footwear.

   These all seem too Dune-inspired(?) --- not much like the way sand 
behaves in real life. Remember, a patch of sand in the dungeon isn't the
same thing as a whole planet full of sand.

>> Digging with tools would not be possible.  "The sand keeps flowing back
>> into the hole" -- but a nice method for hungering yourself.
>> Digging with a wand would cause blindness. "The sand explodes in your
>> face". Digging with a wand would not always be successful.
>> Engraving in sand is not possible.
>> LOTS of scorpions. And don't forget the long worms.
>> "You hear a sound like whispering"
>> Items dropped on sand may get lost.
>> Drum sand -- "The ground reverberates under your feet" (for those not
>> familiar with Dune this sound attracts worms)
>> Stealth is canceled by walking/jumping across sand.
>
> This is brutal. What good does it do the player, except maybe for
> hungering if there's a valuable, edible corpse nearby?

   Hang around sand long enough, and you're guaranteed a worm tooth.
This isn't much of an incentive, though.
   Things leave footprints in sand. Therefore, any non-visible monster 
walking on sand appears as 'I'. Levitating over sand or jumping across
sand wouldn't show anything, of course.
   I like the "kicking sand" idea. It might be something for imps to 
do besides insult the player, too. :)
   Sand would be an obvious place to dig for buried treasure chests, if
the Pirate Patch is also present. (And if digging in sand is allowed to
work, of course.)
   Maybe puddings and jellies would dislike moving over itchy sand, thus 
allowing sand to be used as a barrier. Ditto fog clouds and water demons
and elementals. (A barrier to water demons might be a boon to a low-level 
character, but then sand would have to appear pretty early in the 
dungeon.)
   Quicksand traps. (Okay, so that's another brutal part.)
   Maybe sand should attract sandestins, too, just for the pun value. That 
might be too evil.
   If digging in sand is allowed, and pits "remember" their original
terrain type, then climbing out of a sand pit should be very difficult.
   Potential YAFMs related to "sandbox," "sandpaper."

